WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.024
[MUSIC]

00:00:09.025 --> 00:00:12.535
SPEAKER: How much money should be
spent on reducing air pollution?

00:00:12.548 --> 00:00:16.773
The US Environmental
Protection Agency, the EPA,

00:00:16.773 --> 00:00:20.733
tried to answer this question
with a cost-benefit analysis.

00:00:20.733 --> 00:00:24.793
It asked, how many lives
would be saved by cleaner air,

00:00:24.819 --> 00:00:28.051
and how much was each life worth?

00:00:28.051 --> 00:00:32.109
It set the value of
a life at $3.7 million,

00:00:32.109 --> 00:00:36.612
except for people older
than 70, whose lives it

00:00:36.612 --> 00:00:40.889
valued at only $2.3 million dollars.

00:00:40.889 --> 00:00:46.531
Lying behind the different
valuations was a utilitarian idea.

00:00:46.531 --> 00:00:49.790
Saving an old person's
life produces less utility

00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:54.007
than saving a young person's life
because the young person has longer

00:00:54.007 --> 00:00:59.463
to live, and therefore, more
happiness still to enjoy.

00:00:59.463 --> 00:01:02.403
Advocates for the elderly did not agree.

00:01:02.404 --> 00:01:06.691
They protested what they called
the senior citizen discount.

00:01:06.691 --> 00:01:08.581
What do you think?

00:01:08.581 --> 00:01:14.675
Was the agency right or wrong to
assign a monetary value to human life?

00:01:14.675 --> 00:01:20.248
[MUSIC]

